                emoji: "📸",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Jung Drew the Map. Neuroscience Found the Territory.", body: "Carl Jung described the Shadow in 1912 — decades before brain imaging existed. He said: there's a layer of your psyche containing repressed emotions, disowned traits, and suppressed instincts. It operates outside conscious awareness. It influences your behavior through projection and repetition.\n\nHe was a psychiatrist who collected thousands of case studies. He didn't have fMRI. He didn't have EEG. He had patients, notebooks, and pattern recognition.\n\nNow we have the scanners. And here's what they show:\n\nEverything Jung described has a neural correlate.\n\nNot \"proof\" of every mystical detail — Jung worked pre-technology. But striking predictive alignment. His practical methods work. Brain science now shows us WHY they work.\n\nThis module maps five core Jungian concepts to peer-reviewed neuroscience. No belief required. Just data matching predictions made a century ago."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Shadow = Implicit Memory + Amygdala-Prefrontal Mismatch", body: "JUNG'S MODEL\nThe Shadow = repressed material stored outside ego awareness. It influences behavior through projections (seeing your disowned traits in others) and autonomous complexes (emotional reactions that seem to \"take over\").\n\nNEUROSCIENCE\nSuppressed material doesn't disappear. It gets stored in subcortical networks — the amygdala (threat detection), basal ganglia (habit loops), and insula (body sensation). These are implicit memory systems — they store information you can't consciously access but that still drives behavior.\n\nStudy: Ochsner & Gross (2005)\nSuppressed emotions INCREASE amygdala reactivity while REDUCING prefrontal cortex regulation. The more you suppress, the more reactive your threat system becomes AND the less your rational brain can control it. Literally the opposite of what suppression is supposed to achieve.\n\nStudy: Etkin et al. (2015)\nChronic emotional suppression shows measurable amygdala hyperactivity on fMRI. The shadow is not metaphorical. It's a persistent pattern of neural firing that strengthens over time.\n\nPREDICTIVE CODING (Friston, 2010; Clark, 2013)\nYour brain runs a Bayesian model of the world — constantly generating predictions and comparing them to incoming data. When there's a mismatch, it generates a prediction error.\n\nUnintegrated shadow material = mismatched priors. Your model says \"anger = danger\" (written at age 7), so anyone who shows assertiveness generates a prediction error → amygdala fires → you perceive THREAT where none exists.\n\nThat's projection. In Bayesian terms: a false-positive inference caused by outdated training data.\n\nIntegration = updating the model. Reducing prediction error. Better calibrated priors = fewer false positives = less anxiety, less projection, better decision-making.\n\nStudy: Schwartz & Sweezy (2019) — Internal Family Systems\n\"Exiled\" emotional parts (shadow material) have measurable autonomic signatures — changes in heart rate variability and skin conductance when activated. Shadow parts are real subsystems with real physiological markers."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Why the 90-Second Hold Works — Neurologically", body: "Remember the 90-second exposure technique from Module 7?\n\n1. Feel the emotion without adding narrative\n2. Hold for 90 seconds\n3. Let the chemical cascade complete\n\nHere's the neuroscience:\n\nNeuroanatomist Jill Bolte Taylor demonstrated that the chemical lifespan of an emotion in the body is approximately 90 seconds. The amygdala fires, neurochemicals flood the system (cortisol, adrenaline for fear/anger; or serotonin drop for sadness), and then — if you don't re-trigger the cycle with a story — the chemicals metabolize.\n\nIf the emotion lasts longer than 90 seconds, you're re-triggering it with narrative. \"They shouldn't have said that.\" \"This always happens to me.\" \"I'm not good enough.\" Each thought re-fires the amygdala, generating a fresh chemical cascade.\n\nThe 90-second hold breaks the loop. The amygdala fires. You observe without narrative. The chemicals clear. The prefrontal cortex re-engages. The prediction model UPDATES.\n\nIn clinical terms, this is memory reconsolidation — the same mechanism used in PTSD therapy (Ecker et al., 2012). The memory is activated, held without the old reaction pattern, and the brain rewrites the emotional tag.\n\nJung called it integration.\nNeuroscience calls it reconsolidation.\nSame fix. Different lab."),
