                emoji: "🔧",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Jung's Most Controversial Claim — Validated", body: "Archetypes were always Jung's hardest sell. Universal patterns in a \"collective unconscious\"? Hero, Shadow, Great Mother, Wise Old Man, Trickster — shared by all humans regardless of culture?\n\nSounded like mysticism. Even psychologists resisted it.\n\nThen evolutionary neuropsychology arrived.\n\nStudy: Jaak Panksepp — Affective Neuroscience\nPanksepp identified 7 primary emotional systems conserved across ALL mammals:\n\n• SEEKING — Drive to explore, achieve, discover\n• RAGE — Response to frustration and boundary violation\n• FEAR — Threat detection and avoidance\n• LUST — Sexual drive and bonding\n• CARE — Nurturing, attachment, protection\n• PANIC/GRIEF — Separation distress, loss response\n• PLAY — Social bonding, creativity, joy\n\nThese are not learned. They're hard-wired subcortical circuits. They operate below conscious awareness. They're conserved across species — rats, dogs, cats, primates, humans. Same circuits. Same behaviors.\n\nNow map them to Jung:\n• SEEKING → Hero archetype (drive to explore and achieve)\n• RAGE → Shadow (suppressed aggression and boundary assertion)\n• CARE → Great Mother archetype (nurturing, bonding)\n• FEAR/PANIC → Abandoned Child archetype\n• PLAY → Trickster/Divine Child archetype\n\nJung was describing evolutionary firmware. He just didn't have the vocabulary for it yet.\n\nStudy: Knox (2003)\nReframed archetypes as early attachment schemas wired into orbitofrontal and cingulate circuits. Your brain comes pre-loaded with templates for how to relate to the world — parent, protector, threat, ally, lover. These templates activate automatically in social situations and shape behavior before conscious thought engages."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Cross-Cultural Proof", body: "If archetypes are real neural hardware, they should show up identically across cultures with no contact. They do.\n\nStudy: Ekman — Universal Facial Expressions\nSeven basic facial expressions (happiness, sadness, anger, fear, disgust, surprise, contempt) are recognized universally across all studied cultures, including isolated indigenous populations with no media exposure. Same expressions. Same recognition. Same neural activation patterns.\n\nStudy: Whalen et al. — Universal Threat Processing\nFearful faces activate the amygdala identically across cultures, even when presented subliminally (below conscious awareness). The hardware doesn't care about your cultural software. It fires the same way everywhere.\n\nStudy: Campbell (1949) → Neuroaesthetics (2010s-2020s)\nJoseph Campbell documented the \"Hero's Journey\" — the same narrative arc appearing in myths across every culture on Earth. Modern neuroaesthetics shows that hero-journey narratives reliably activate reward circuits (ventral striatum) and coherence circuits (40 Hz gamma binding) regardless of cultural context.\n\nThe story template is built into the brain. Every culture discovers it because every brain contains it.\n\nPRACTICAL IMPLICATION\nArchetypes aren't mystical entities. They're evolved subroutines — default behavioral templates your brain ships with. When you notice yourself stuck in a Hero loop (\"I must save everyone\"), a Victim loop (\"this always happens to me\"), or a Savior loop (\"they need me\") — that's firmware executing.\n\nYou can't delete firmware. But you can become aware of when it's running and choose whether to follow its default behavior or override it with conscious input.\n\nSpotting archetypes = recognizing which default subroutine is currently executing in your decision engine."),
